== Boundary and disposition

Fresh Atom admission first checks syntax eligibility. Numbers, opaque variables,
and recursively eligible ordinary scalar algebra bypass discovery. Spenso tags,
chain placeholders, Spenso-owned function heads, callbacks, evaluation hooks and
unnormalized syntax take full admission. Both paths publish through the existing
`from_validated_parts` owner. The shortcut supplies empty construction history,
sets the current intrinsic, normalized-product, rewrite and algebra facts, and
never reinterprets an existing tensor carrier from its printed payload.

The full route remains nested-vector lowering, tensor-power lowering, observed
scope/signature/history validation, interface merging and product normalization.
The last shared input is the fresh Atom; the first different boundary is syntax
eligibility versus discovery. Tests compare payload bytes, logical layout,
carrier flags, contraction reports, derived facts and retained index entries.
Typed cancellation tests separately protect a zero payload with retained ports.

The source's syntax predicate and equivalence matrix are ported. Its direct
obsolete proof initializer is replaced by current metadata publication. Fixed-key
cache hashing and lazy fresh-index allocation are deferred: these are separate
performance changes to shared inference state, not prerequisites for eligibility.
They are not claimed already satisfied. Current upstream already owns observed
history publication and callback/typed-zero preservation; those owners are reused.
No planner, transport order, template controls, lockfiles or snapshots change.

== Validation

The structured receipt beside the integration evidence records exact checks and
limitations. The existing thread-local `INFERENCE_CALLS` counter compares the
same coupling product through shortcut and full admission: zero versus one
interface-discovery call, with identical expression and structure. This proves
skipped discovery only; it makes no end-to-end speedup claim.
